# HQL-0062: against other script languages

Companion to [[HQL-0062]]. Python, Node, Bash, Ruby, Perl and Lua share
one command-line shape for running a program: a file and its arguments, an
inline program after a flag, standard input when it is piped, and an
interactive prompt on a terminal. This note compares `hql` with them point
by point. Where `hql` follows them it says so. Where it does not, it names
the reason and the place that owns the rule.

!!! abstract "TL;DR"
    - **The shared convention is followed as is.** No arguments on a
      terminal is the REPL, piped standard input is a program, `FILE ARG...`
      runs the file with the words after it as its arguments, options after
      `FILE` are the script's, and `-c PROGRAM ARG...` runs an inline
      program with arguments.
    - **Five points differ**, each for a stated reason: a free word is an
      expression, a word is a file only when written as a path, `argv()`
      leaves out the program's own path, the exit status comes from the
      result, and a relative path resolves beside the script.

## The comparison

| | Python | Node | Bash | Ruby | Perl | Lua | `hql` |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| No arguments, a terminal | REPL | REPL | interactive shell | reads a program from the terminal; `irb` is the REPL | reads a program from the terminal | REPL | REPL |
| No arguments, piped input | runs it | runs it | runs it | runs it | runs it | runs it | runs it, as `hql run -` |
| `X FILE A B` | runs `FILE`; `sys.argv[1:]` is `A B` | runs `FILE`; `process.argv[2:]` is `A B` | runs `FILE`; `$@` is `A B` | runs `FILE`; `ARGV` is `A B` | runs `FILE`; `@ARGV` is `A B` | runs `FILE`; `arg[1]`, `arg[2]` are `A B` | runs `FILE`; `argv()` is `["A", "B"]` |
| An option after `FILE` | the script's | the script's | the script's | the script's | the script's | the script's | the script's |
| Inline program | `-c` | `-e`, `-p` | `-c` | `-e` | `-e` | `-e` | `-c`, or `eval` |
| Arguments after an inline program | yes; `sys.argv[0]` is `-c` | yes | yes; the first is `$0` | yes | yes | no; the first word is a script | yes; `argv()` is every word after the program |
| `X WORD`, `WORD` no file | fails: no such file | fails: cannot find module | fails: no such file | fails: no such file | fails: no such file | fails: cannot open | evaluates `WORD` as an expression |
| What counts as a file | the first free word | the first free word | the first free word | the first free word | the first free word | the first free word | a word ending in `.hql` or containing `/` |
| The program's own path | `sys.argv[0]` | `process.argv[1]` | `$0` | `$0` | `$0` | `arg[0]` | not in `argv()`; `fs.source_dir()` and the file's name; the process itself is `io.process` ([[HQL-0064]]) |
| Exit status | an uncaught exception `1`; `sys.exit(n)` | an uncaught error `1`; `process.exit(n)` | the last command's; `exit n` | an uncaught exception `1`; `exit(n)` | `die` non-zero, `255` when no error code is set; `exit(n)` | an error `1`; `os.exit(n)` | a failure or an `err` result `1`, else `0`; no operation sets it |
| A relative path | the working directory | the working directory (`fs`) | the working directory | the working directory | the working directory | the working directory | the script's folder |

Deno follows the same convention with named commands, the shape `hql`
has: `deno run FILE`, `deno eval PROGRAM` and `deno repl`, with
`deno FILE` as a shorthand for `deno run FILE` and `deno` alone as the REPL.
`hql run`, `hql eval` and `hql repl` stay, and `hql FILE`, `hql -c` and
`hql` alone are their shorthands.

## Where `hql` differs, and why

- **A free word is an expression.** `hql '1 + 1'` prints `2`, as `jq` and
  `awk` take their program as the first word. That form predates scripts
  ([[HQL-0025]]), and every invocation that worked keeps working.
- **A word is a file only when it is written as one**, with `.hql` or a
  `/`. A `#!` invocation always carries a `/`, so every script runs. A bare
  word stays an expression even when a file of that name exists, so a file
  placed in the working directory cannot turn a typed expression into a
  program run (the section "`hql FILE`" of [[HQL-0062]]).
- **`argv()` leaves out the program's own path.** It is the arguments
  only, as `sys.argv[1:]` and `$@` are. The path is already known as
  `fs.source_dir()` and the file's name, and an inline program has none.
  `io.env` holds what the invocation fixed before the program started.
  What is true of the running process, its identifier, its parent and its
  signals, is `io.process` ([[HQL-0064]], the note on `io.process`).
- **The exit status comes from the result.** HQL has no `exit(n)`: a
  failure is a value ([[HQL-0033]]), and an operation that ends the process
  would be a jump out of an evaluation. A result that is `err(e)` exits `1`,
  as an uncaught exception does in Python, Node and Ruby. The companion note
  [pure and effectful](01-pure-and-effectful.hmd) gives the reasoning.
- **A relative path resolves against the script's folder.** One rule for
  every unit ([[HQL-0059]], [[HQL-0040]]) keeps a query's answer
  independent of where it is run. A script reaches the directory it was run
  from with `fs.working_dir()`, and joins a path from `argv()` to it:
  `working_dir().join(p)`, which gives `p` itself when `p` is absolute.
  The note [relative paths, in a script and in a cell](03-relative-paths.hmd)
  states what the rule gives a cell, and why notebook tools follow it too.
